From 16331b64cd0c41e1438ac491829cb8e642eacd1f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 19:26:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: `{fd}>x` and `>&`/`<&`/`>|`
 redirection words no longer hide the command
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Card t_01a1d5f876488f96 (found while rebasing PR #2166). `scan_gateway_lifecycle`
returned (False, None) while bash ran the restart script for:

1. bash's descriptor-variable redirections `{NAME}>x`, `{NAME}<x`, `{NAME}>&N`,
   `{NAME}>|x`, `{NAME}<<<x` ... before the command word, between a wrapper
   and its program, or among the program's operands (`{fd}>/dev/null bash
   r.sh`, `env {fd}>&2 bash r.sh`, `bash {fd}>/dev/null r.sh`).
   `split_glued_redirections` split `{fd}>x` into a command word `{fd}` and
   `>x`, and `_REDIRECTION` only knew a numeric descriptor.
2. Any `>&`, `<&` or `>|` (`2>&1 bash r.sh`, `>&2 bash r.sh`, `bash 2>&1
   r.sh`, `2>|log bash r.sh`): the tokenizer's punctuation set cut the word at
   `&`/`|`, so the line became `2>` and a new command `1 bash r.sh` (or a pipe
   into `/tmp/log`).

Fix (both only add redirection recognition; quoted/escaped text is unchanged):
- `cron/lifecycle_guard_groups.split_glued_redirections` leaves a whole
  `{NAME}` word glued to its operator (`FD_VARIABLE`), and escapes the `&`/`|`
  of an unquoted, unescaped `>&`, `<&`, `>|` so shlex keeps the operator one
  word. A quoted or escaped `>` (`echo '2>'& …`, `echo \>& …`) is text, so its
  `&` stays a control operator.
- `cron/lifecycle_guard._REDIRECTION` accepts `{NAME}` as the descriptor, so
  the command-position hoist and `_split_redirections` treat `{fd}>x` like
  `2>x` (`{fd}<f` is not stdin).

Tests:
- New tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_fd_redirections.py: 24 shapes real bash
  runs (each asserts the marker, then a refusal), 10 that stay allowed
  (`{fd}>/dev/null python3 clean.py`, `python3 {fd}>/dev/null clean.py`,
  `{1a}>x`, `q{fd}>x`, quoted/escaped `>&`), and the tokenizer split table.
- Corpus (tests/cron/heredoc_shape_corpus.py): fourth family
  `s_position == "redirect"`: 14 redirection words ({fd} forms plus numeric
  `>&`/`>|` controls) x place (lead / after wrapper / among operands) x every
  WRAPPER x nesting (plain, function, subshell, outer heredoc) x target
  (`bash ./restart.sh`, `bash < ./restart.sh`, clean `python3 ./clean.py`).
  Real bash runs each; a run case must be refused whatever main says, a clean
  case must not be refused (`false_refusals`, also counted by `report`). The
  sample covers every prefix x place x target, prefix x wrapper, prefix x
  nesting. main_verdicts.json regenerated from main (now 1d9baebbd5; it also
  picks up main's own verdict changes since da52aff8f4 in the older families).

Review t_01a1d641741f64ff (request_changes on e3dcae98db): bash 5.2 also
takes an array element as the descriptor variable (`{a[1]}>/dev/null bash
r.sh`, `env {m[k]}>&2 bash r.sh`, `{a[b[1]]}>x`, `{m["k k"]}>x`); those were
still allowed. Now:
- `FD_VARIABLE` and `_REDIRECTION` accept an optional non-empty `[subscript]`
  (`{a[]}` is not one: bash runs it as a command).
- A subscript the tokenizer cuts (`{a[$((1+1))]}>x`, `{a[$(echo 1)]}>x`,
  ``{a[`echo 1`]}>x``) fails closed: the word ending in `]}` glued to the
  operator becomes `{_}`. The subscript's own `$(…)` stays on the line, so
  `{a[$(bash r.sh)]}>x echo` is still refused.
- Not fixed here (pre-existing on main, a wider class): a `$(…)`/`$((…))`/
  backtick inside ANY word cuts the command into segments, so e.g.
  `bash 2>$(echo /dev/null) < ./r.sh` and `bash {a[$((1+1))]}>x < ./r.sh`
  still fail open. Follow-up card t_01a1d6502aa80fb5 owns that.
- Tests: 14 more RUN rows (array elements, cut subscripts), 4 more allowed
  rows, 7 more split-table rows. Corpus adds `{a[1]}>/dev/null` and
  `{m[k]}>&2` prefixes (16 redirection words).

Review t_01a1d6855e13d1d2 (request_changes on f6d793a104): the `;` that
ended a command at an unterminated `{NAME[` head cut by a space detached a
later stdin redirection, so `bash -s {a[x < ./r.sh` (and `sudo bash -s …`,
`sh -s …`, `bash -s {a[1 2]} < ./r.sh`, …) ran the restart and was allowed;
the previous head refused them. Now:
- No `;` at a head. A head is recorded and only used when a word ending in
  `]}` is glued to `<`/`>` later in the same command: the span from the
  first head to that tail becomes `{_}` (one descriptor-variable word, as
  bash reads `{a[`echo 1`]}>x`). Without such a tail the head is an
  ordinary word and the line is unchanged (`echo {a[x bash ./r.sh` is no
  longer a false refusal).
- The rewritten text is no longer dropped (review nit: `echo
  "$(bash ./r.sh)"]}>/dev/null` became `echo {_}>/dev/null`): it becomes a
  command of its own at the end of the line, before a `#` comment, or at its
  start when the line ends in a control operator, so it adds no operand
  (`bash < f {a[…` would read a script file instead of stdin) and cuts no
  pipe. `bash -s {a[`echo 1`]}>/dev/null < ./r.sh`, allowed at f6d793a104,
  is refused.
- Tests: 13 RUN rows (the review's 10 shapes plus `{a[1 2]}>x`, the cut
  subscript before a stdin redirection and in a pipe), 1 allowed row,
  8 split-table rows. Corpus: prefixes ``{a[`echo 1`]}>&2``, `{a[x`,
  `{a[1 2]}` (plain words: bash runs the payload only as a `bash -s`
  operand, asserted) and a `bash -s < ./restart.sh` target.
- Backticks anywhere (`echo `bash ./r.sh``) and `"$(…)"` are not scanned on
  main either; t_01a1d6502aa80fb5 owns that class.

Proof (verdicts/corpus on origin/main 0324054f56; then rebased on 6af4055128):
- `heredoc_shape_corpus.py report --full`: this tree cases=28292
  bash_ran=11190 violations=0 false_refusals=0 (the first version of this
  fix, which appended the text as operands, had 80 violations there).
  main_verdicts.json regenerated from 0324054f56.
- hermes-core-wt test tests/cron/ + every other test importing the guard:
  240 files, 3291 passed, 1 failed (the corpus run-assertion, fixed for the
  plain-word prefixes; rerun: test_lifecycle_guard_heredoc_walk.py 253 passed)
- scripts/check --base origin/main (6af4055128): 20 checks ok.
